/**
\defgroup elementary Elementary functions
\brief Exponential function \f$\exp(x)\f$ and natural logarithm \f$\log(x)\f$.

This module provides \f$\exp(x)\f$ and \f$\log(x)\f$ in `<laplace/math/elementary.hpp>`. Functions are marked `LAPLACE_HD`, run on the host and inside Kokkos kernels on the device, and are called as `laplace::math::exp` and `laplace::math::log`. Under default dispatch, `exp()` is accurate to at most 1.0000 ulp on CPU and GPU, and `log()` is accurate to at most 0.7767 ulp on CPU and 0.5207 ulp on CUDA.

## Definitions

Here \f$x\f$ is the input argument. The function \ref laplace::math::exp "exp()" computes \f$\exp(x)\f$, and \ref laplace::math::log "log()" computes \f$\log(x)\f$. The header also holds the underlying candidate implementations in `laplace::math::own` and `laplace::math::platform`, which have no separate entries.

## Domain and special values

Defined on \f$x \in \mathbb{R}\f$, `exp()` equals \f$1.0\f$ at \f$0.0\f$, \f$+\infty\f$ at \f$+\infty\f$, and \f$0.0\f$ at \f$-\infty\f$, returning quiet NaN for NaN inputs. It overflows to \f$+\infty\f$ for \f$x > 709.782712893383973096\f$ and underflows to \f$0.0\f$ for \f$x < -745.133219101941108420\f$. On the host in the default configuration, arguments satisfying \f$|x| < 2^{-28}\f$ (\f$\approx 3.72529 \times 10^{-9}\f$) return \f$1.0 + x\f$.

Defined on \f$x \in (0, \infty)\f$, `log()` satisfies \f$\log(1.0) = 0.0\f$ and \f$\log(+\infty) = +\infty\f$, and returns \f$-\infty\f$ at \f$\pm 0.0\f$. Negative inputs (\f$x < 0.0\f$) and NaN inputs return quiet NaN. Subnormal arguments (\f$x < 2^{-1022}\f$) are supported down to \f$5 \times 10^{-324}\f$.

## Method

The public wrappers `laplace::math::exp` and `laplace::math::log` select between two implementations depending on the execution space and build configuration. On host execution spaces (CPU), calls invoke `laplace::math::own` by default so every CPU platform runs the same algorithm. This avoids the roughly 28 ns per call cost of the Windows Rtools GCC standard library. Device calls invoke `laplace::math::platform` (CUDA libdevice via Kokkos; Trott and others, 2022), and defining `LAPLACE_PLATFORM_ELEMENTARY` (CMake option `LAPLACE_OWN_ELEMENTARY=OFF`) routes host calls to `platform` too.

The `own` implementations follow the table-free fdlibm 5.3 algorithms (SunSoft, Sun Microsystems, 1993). On the host, `own::exp` reduces the argument as \f$x = k \ln 2 + r\f$ with \f$k \in \mathbb{Z}\f$ and \f$|r| \le \frac{\ln 2}{2}\f$. It splits \f$\ln 2\f$ into exact high and low double-precision parts to avoid cancellation (Cody and Waite, 1980). On the reduced interval, it evaluates a degree-5 minimax rational approximation in \f$r^2\f$ and scales by \f$2^k\f$ in the IEEE 754 exponent field (IEEE, 2019). For \f$k < -1021\f$, it scales in two steps to evaluate subnormal results without premature underflow.

For the logarithm, `own::log` scales subnormal inputs (\f$x < 2^{-1022}\f$) by \f$2^{54}\f$ and normalizes positive inputs to \f$x = 2^k(1 + f)\f$ with \f$1 + f \in [\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}, \sqrt{2})\f$. When \f$-2^{-20} \le f < 2^{-20}\f$, it evaluates a degree-3 Taylor polynomial in \f$f\f$; otherwise it transforms to \f$s = f / (2 + f)\f$ and evaluates a degree-7 minimax polynomial in \f$s^2\f$. The final sum adds \f$k \ln 2\f$ using the split high and low parts of \f$\ln 2\f$.

## Accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for error metrics and reference generation. Errors below are plain relative ulp measured against 200-bit `mpmath` (2023) double-double references generated by `tools/reference/elementary.py`.

| Function | Execution space and candidate | Max error (ulp) | Correctly rounded share |
|---|---|---|---|
| \ref laplace::math::exp "exp()" | CPU host (`own`, gcc and MSVC) | 1.0000 | 0.9270 (gcc) |
| \ref laplace::math::exp "exp()" | GPU device (`platform`, CUDA) | 1.0000 (0.7930 normal) | 0.9498 |
| \ref laplace::math::log "log()" | CPU host (`own`, gcc and MSVC) | 0.7767 | 0.9799 (gcc) |
| \ref laplace::math::log "log()" | GPU device (`platform`, CUDA) | 0.5207 | 0.9988 |

When `nvcc` does not contract multiply-add operations into FMA (`--fmad=false`, CMake option `LAPLACE_CUDA_FMAD=OFF`), `laplace::math::own` yields bit-identical results on gcc, MSVC, and CUDA on the reference arguments. See `docs/numerics.md` for comparisons across `own` and `platform` on all backends and bit-level reproducibility counts.

## Speed

Timings are measured over \f$2^{23}\f$ arguments in `benchmarks/math_elementary.cpp` for the default configuration. On CPU, `exp()` takes 6.4 ns per call on one gcc thread, 0.65 ns with 16 OpenMP threads, and 8.1 ns on one MSVC host thread. On CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER), it takes 0.070 ns per call. On CPU, `log()` takes 4.1 ns per call on one gcc thread, 0.45 ns with 16 OpenMP threads, and 5.0 ns on one MSVC host thread. On CUDA, it takes 0.131 ns per call.

## Example

The following example evaluates \f$\exp(x)\f$ and \f$\log(x)\f$ inside a `Kokkos::parallel_for` kernel:

\code{.cpp}
#include <laplace/math/elementary.hpp>
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>

void evaluate_elementary(const Kokkos::View<const double*>& x,
                         Kokkos::View<double*>& y_exp,
                         Kokkos::View<double*>& y_log,
                         int n) {
  Kokkos::parallel_for("eval_elementary", n, KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    const double val = x(i);
    y_exp(i) = laplace::math::exp(val);
    y_log(i) = laplace::math::log(val);
  });
}
\endcode

## Module functions

Functions in this module are called as `laplace::math::NAME`.

- \ref laplace::math::exp "exp()" — exponential function \f$\exp(x)\f$
- \ref laplace::math::log "log()" — natural logarithm \f$\log(x)\f$

## References

- W. J. Cody and W. Waite (1980), Software Manual for the Elementary Functions, Prentice-Hall.
- IEEE (2019), IEEE Standard for Floating-Point Arithmetic, IEEE Std 754-2019. https://doi.org/10.1109/IEEESTD.2019.8766229
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
- Sun Microsystems (1993), fdlibm: freely distributable C math library, version 5.3, https://www.netlib.org/fdlibm/
- C. R. Trott and others (2022), Kokkos 3: Programming Model Extensions for the Exascale Era, IEEE Transactions on Parallel and Distributed Systems, 33(4), 805–817. https://doi.org/10.1109/tpds.2021.3097283
*/
